<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Appalachian Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Appalachians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Appalachian Mountains are an ancient mountain system of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, stretching about 3,200 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>. For British colonists they formed a barrier to westward settlement, marked by the Proclamation Line of 1763, until settlers crossed into Kentucky and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> through passes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> Gap. The southern Appalachians were the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>, and the region later became a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-mining area.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains.jpg" alt="Appalachian Mountains" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J Guth · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue_Ridge_Parkway_Sunset_(43691100).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Appalachians extend from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> along a southwest-northeast axis. The highest peak is Mount Mitchell, 2,037 metres, in the Black Mountains of North Carolina, part of the Blue Ridge Mountains. Saint Pierre and Miquelon, an overseas collectivity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, is part of the range, so it technically spans three countries. The range is geologically related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-highlands">Scottish Highlands</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas Mountains</a>, the Scandinavian Mountains and the Ouachitas, which all once belonged to the Central Pangean Mountains.</p><p>Their formation began about 1.1 billion years ago with the creation of the supercontinent Rodinia, when the Grenville orogeny built mountains whose rocks remain in the Blue Ridge and the Adirondacks. Rodinia later broke up and the Iapetus Ocean formed, precursor of the Atlantic, where the rocks of the Valley and Ridge province were laid down. The modern Appalachians arose from three orogenies beginning about 480 million years ago, making them one of the oldest surviving mountain ranges. They once reached elevations similar to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and Rockies before 240 million years of erosion wore them down to the lower, rugged peaks of today.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name comes from the village the Narváez expedition, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvar-nunez-cabeza-de-vaca">Álvar Núñez Cabeza de Vaca</a>, found near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tallahassee">Tallahassee</a> in 1528 and transcribed as Apalchen. The Spanish altered it to Apalachee and used it for the tribe and the region spreading inland to the north; it is the fourth-oldest surviving European place-name in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a>'s expedition of 1540 Spanish cartographers began to apply the name to the mountains; the first use for the range is on Jacques le Moyne de Morgues's map of 1565. The name was not commonly used for the whole range until the late 19th century, and Allegheny Mountains was often more popular.</p><p>The ridges and valleys run against most east-west highways and railroads, so the range was a barrier to east-west travel that was extremely important in shaping the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the colonial era. Much of its economy was historically based on logging and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining, which attracted many migrants who contributed to a new cultural region. Many communities have since moved into manufacturing and services.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The range holds a wide variety of biomes, from temperate broadleaf and mixed forest in the south to boreal forest in the north. Red spruce and balsam fir dominate the north and high elevations, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> maple and beech are more common further south. The Appalachians, particularly the central and southern parts, are among the most biodiverse places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Tourism is prominent, above all the Appalachian Trail, a hiking trail of 3,500 km from Mount Katahdin in Maine to Springer Mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Appalachian Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cherokee, in their own language Tsalagi, lived in towns across the southern <b>Appalachians</b>, in parts of present-day Georgia, Tennessee, North Carolina, South Carolina and Alabama.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Haudenosaunee backed the British in the French and Indian War, and the Royal Proclamation of 1763 forbade white settlement beyond the <b>Appalachians</b>, an order the colonists largely ignored.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>When colonial settlers first crossed the <b>Appalachians</b> they were astonished by the earthworks.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hopewell-culture">Hopewell culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Craftsmen hammered copper from the Great Lakes and the <b>Appalachians</b> into thin sheets and embossed them with figures such as the winged falcon dancers on the plates from Etowah, which are thought to have been made at Cahokia in the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Jean Ritchie, a musician from the <b>Appalachian Mountains</b>, was recorded by Alan Lomax in 1949 singing a version learned from her Kentucky family, whose ancestors seemingly brought it from England; she recorded it again on her 1959 album Carols of All Seasons.</blockquote><a href="/songs/i-saw-three-ships">I Saw Three Ships</a></li><li><blockquote>(The Shakers had once worshipped on Holy Mount, in the Massachusetts part of the <b>Appalachians</b>.)</blockquote><a href="/songs/simple-gifts">Simple Gifts</a></li></ul></section>
